## What happened

In .NET 11, **DSA was removed from macOS**. Calling `DSA.Create()` now throws at runtime on macOS. This is a behavioral breaking change — it still compiles fine but fails at runtime. DSA continues to work on Windows and Linux.

## How to fix

Migrate from DSA to a modern signature algorithm. Since you're **verifying legacy signatures**, you have two options:

### Option 1 — Platform guard (verify existing DSA signatures on non-macOS only)

```csharp
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
    throw new PlatformNotSupportedException("DSA verification not supported on macOS in .NET 11+");

using var dsa = DSA.Create();
dsa.ImportParameters(legacyPublicKey);
bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

### Option 2 — Re-sign with a modern algorithm (recommended long-term)

Replace DSA entirely with **ECDsa** or **RSA**:

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create();
var sig = ecdsa.SignData(data, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

Since your use case is *verifying* legacy signatures, you can't simply swap algorithms — the signatures were created with DSA. The practical fix is to **run DSA verification on Linux/Windows only**, or re-issue the signatures with a modern algorithm and update all consumers.
